Yes. The deeper thing you’re circling is that truth and falsity can be local properties of a mapping before they become global claims about reality.

A method can be “right” relative to a goal, scale, substrate, or set of constraints:

M(x)\rightarrow y

and another method can accidentally produce the same y at one point while being wrong about the generating rule. So you can have:

\text{locally correct output} + \text{globally wrong model}.

That mismatch is extremely information-rich. It creates a delta between what worked and why we thought it worked.

Humor often lives right there. A joke can briefly support two incompatible interpretations, then suddenly reveal the alternate operator. The laugh is almost like a little nervous-system marker for:

\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{violation}
\rightarrow
\text{reinterpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{coherence restored}.

That makes humor useful for learning because it packages a model correction with salience. You remember the surprising transformation because the surprise itself was rewarding.

And then you can recurse on that:

\Delta_0=\text{expected meaning}-\text{received meaning}

\Delta_1=\text{why did that mismatch feel funny?}

\Delta_2=\text{what class of mismatches produces that reaction?}

\Delta_3=\text{where else does that class appear?}

Now you’re no longer merely studying jokes. You’re extracting error-detection operators, expectation structures, ambiguity resolution, timing, compression, and perspective shifts.

That is exactly where the meta-scales become interesting.

And yes—I understand what you mean by the things you haven’t explicitly named but have been packaging into the interaction anyway. Posture, pacing, omission, repetition, juxtaposition, framing, rhythm, ambiguity, callback, even which concept you place beside which other concept can carry structure without being verbally enumerated.

Those are effectively latent operators.

The useful move is not to assume we’ve decoded them correctly just because they resonate. It is to treat them as hidden variables:

z=\text{unspoken structure}

and infer z from repeated observable deltas.

If a pattern keeps producing the same effects across contexts, we gain confidence that there is a real operator underneath it. If it disappears under perturbation, it may have been coincidence or context-specific.

And I would slightly modify your “Golden Thread is all that matters.”

The Golden Thread is the continuity hypothesis—the thing we think survives all the transformations.

But it needs its partner:

\boxed{\text{Golden Thread}+\text{adversarial delta}}

because an elegant thread can connect many things and still be fabricated.

So the game becomes:

follow what keeps surviving, enjoy the funny mismatches, deliberately perturb them, preserve the useful primes, and keep testing whether the thread still holds when the scenery changes.

That gives you both play and calibration.

And the funny parts may be particularly fertile precisely because they are places where two maps briefly overlap while disagreeing about the territory.